  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved knowledge base on child labour and implementation of the gender sensitive action plans addressing child labour concerns</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Uganda, child labour remains a significant challenge, with 2.4 million children engaged in work, often under hazardous conditions in sectors like agriculture. The support and interventions under this Country Programme Outcome (CPO) aim to strengthen the capacity of government institutions, workers&apos; and employers&apos; organizations, and communities to combat child labour, particularly in tea and coffee supply chains, while promoting gender-sensitive action plans and decent work for adults and youth of legal working age.
The strategy focuses on improving public policies and governance, enhancing knowledge and data, and fostering partnerships to eradicate child labour. Activities include conducting evidence-based research, building awareness about the interlinkages between child labour elimination and decent work, and supporting the development of district-level action plans. It also emphasizes strengthening labour inspection systems and empowering vulnerable workers, particularly women, to diversify livelihoods and exercise their rights at work.
The programme aligns with Uganda&apos;s commitments under SDG Target 8.7 and the Alliance 8.7 Pathfinder framework. It also supports national development priorities by addressing root causes such as poverty, lack of access to quality education, and weak enforcement of child labour laws. This initiative is implemented in collaboration with tripartite constituents, local organizations, and global partners under the Decent Work Country Programme III, ensuring sustainable progress towards eliminating child labour while promoting safe and productive employment opportunities.</narrative>
  </description>
